      "y": 591.375,
      "width": 909.3793334960938,
      "height": 1275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 318819256,
      "version": 910,
      "versionNonce": 521797063,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782867643418,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Direct execution on the API server:\nThe user-submitted code is directly executed on the same server that hosts the API. \nThe code is typically saved as a file on the server's filesystem \nand then run using the appropriate interpreter or compiler.\n\nPros:\n-- Simple to implement\n-- Low latency as there's no additional layer between the API and code execution\nCons:\n-- Severe security risks: Malicious code can potentially access or modify server data\n-- Poor isolation: A bug in user code could crash the entire server\n-- Resource management issues: One user's code could consume all server resources\n======================================\n\n2) Virtual Machine (VM) -- isolate execution in virtualized environment. Code run inside\nisolated environment, which emulates dedicate hardware\nPros:\n-- Strong isolation: Each execution environment is completely separate\n-- Flexible: Can run any operating system or configuration\n-- Good security: VM has limited access to host system\nCons:\n-- Resource intensive: Each VM requires significant CPU, memory, and storage\n-- Slow to start: Booting a full OS takes time\n-- Complex to manage: Requires expertise in virtualization technologies\n=======================================\n\n3) Docker -- lightweight, isolated execution environments ==> offer good balance of security,\nperformance and resource efficiency\nShared host OS kernel but run in isolated user spaces, providing lightweight, consistent env.\nPros:\n-- Efficient resource use: Lighter than VMs, allowing more concurrent executions\n-- Fast startup: Containers can start in seconds\n-- Consistent environment: Ensures code runs the same way in development and production\n-- Good isolation: Processes in one container can't directly affect others\nCons:\n-- Less isolation than VMs: All containers share the host OS kernel\n-- Potential security issues if not properly configured\n-- Learning curve for container technologies\n===========================================\n\n4) Serverless: event-driven, auto scaling execution ==> great for short run\nPros:\n-- Highly scalable: Automatically handles varying loads\n-- Cost-effective: Pay only for actual compute time used\n-- Low maintenance: Provider manages the infrastructure\n-- Good isolation: Each function execution is independent\nCons:\n-- \"Cold start latency\": Infrequently used functions may have delayed startup\n-- Limited execution time: Most providers have maximum runtime limits\n-- Potential vendor lock-in: May be tied to specific cloud provider services\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Direct execution on the API server:\nThe user-submitted code is directly executed on the same server that hosts the API. \nThe code is typically saved as a file on the server's filesystem \nand then run using the appropriate interpreter or compiler.\n\nPros:\n-- Simple to implement\n-- Low latency as there's no additional layer between the API and code execution\nCons:\n-- Severe security risks: Malicious code can potentially access or modify server data\n-- Poor isolation: A bug in user code could crash the entire server\n-- Resource management issues: One user's code could consume all server resources\n======================================\n\n2) Virtual Machine (VM) -- isolate execution in virtualized environment. Code run inside\nisolated environment, which emulates dedicate hardware\nPros:\n-- Strong isolation: Each execution environment is completely separate\n-- Flexible: Can run any operating system or configuration\n-- Good security: VM has limited access to host system\nCons:\n-- Resource intensive: Each VM requires significant CPU, memory, and storage\n-- Slow to start: Booting a full OS takes time\n-- Complex to manage: Requires expertise in virtualization technologies\n=======================================\n\n3) Docker -- lightweight, isolated execution environments ==> offer good balance of security,\nperformance and resource efficiency\nShared host OS kernel but run in isolated user spaces, providing lightweight, consistent env.\nPros:\n-- Efficient resource use: Lighter than VMs, allowing more concurrent executions\n-- Fast startup: Containers can start in seconds\n-- Consistent environment: Ensures code runs the same way in development and production\n-- Good isolation: Processes in one container can't directly affect others\nCons:\n-- Less isolation than VMs: All containers share the host OS kernel\n-- Potential security issues if not properly configured\n-- Learning curve for container technologies\n===========================================\n\n4) Serverless: event-driven, auto scaling execution ==> great for short run\nPros:\n-- Highly scalable: Automatically handles varying loads\n-- Cost-effective: Pay only for actual compute time used\n-- Low maintenance: Provider manages the infrastructure\n-- Good isolation: Each function execution is independent\nCons:\n-- \"Cold start latency\": Infrequently used functions may have delayed startup\n-- Limited execution time: Most providers have maximum runtime limits\n-- Potential vendor lock-in: May be tied to specific cloud provider services\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
