    {
      "id": "2gkf0lfU8VdzhCt9Vf2o2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1966.169435420024,
      "y": 739.8389409772528,
      "width": 1219.9791259765625,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b16",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1013788360,
      "version": 90,
      "versionNonce": 1459458983,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782867643418,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** How to receive result?? ==> with polling\n1) Immediate response: Code Evaluation Service returns {\"submission_id\": \"abc123\", \"status\": \"pending\"} in under 100ms\n2) Polling: Client polls GET /submissions/abc123/status every 1-2 seconds\n3) Result delivery: When execution completes, the endpoint returns {\"status\": \"success\", \"passed\": 8, \"failed\": 2, \"time\": 45ms}\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** How to receive result?? ==> with polling\n1) Immediate response: Code Evaluation Service returns {\"submission_id\": \"abc123\", \"status\": \"pending\"} in under 100ms\n2) Polling: Client polls GET /submissions/abc123/status every 1-2 seconds\n3) Result delivery: When execution completes, the endpoint returns {\"status\": \"success\", \"passed\": 8, \"failed\": 2, \"time\": 45ms}\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RnvYuC2Ab1GE3eA58puMJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1966.169435420024,
      "y": 1560.4775065405884,
      "width": 1325.4591064453125,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 901875384,
      "version": 60,
      "versionNonce": 1343495753,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782867643418,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** Why choosing polling?\n-- WebSockets maintain bidirectional connections. During a contest, 10,000 concurrent WebSocket connections \ncreate memory pressure on the server—each connection holds buffers and state. \nWe only need one-way communication (server → client) for status updates. The bidirectional capability adds complexity without benefit.\n\n-- Server-Sent Events (SSE) are one-way but still require long-lived connections. \nWith 10,000 concurrent users, that's 10,000 open HTTP connections the server must maintain.\n\n-- Polling is stateless. The server handles a request, sends a response, and closes the connection.\n No memory held between requests. The \"cost\" of polling—1 request every 1-2 seconds per active submission—\nis negligible compared to maintaining 10,000 persistent connections. LeetCode proves this works at scale.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** Why choosing polling?\n-- WebSockets maintain bidirectional connections. During a contest, 10,000 concurrent WebSocket connections \ncreate memory pressure on the server—each connection holds buffers and state. \nWe only need one-way communication (server → client) for status updates. The bidirectional capability adds complexity without benefit.\n\n-- Server-Sent Events (SSE) are one-way but still require long-lived connections. \nWith 10,000 concurrent users, that's 10,000 open HTTP connections the server must maintain.\n\n-- Polling is stateless. The server handles a request, sends a response, and closes the connection.\n No memory held between requests. The \"cost\" of polling—1 request every 1-2 seconds per active submission—\nis negligible compared to maintaining 10,000 persistent connections. LeetCode proves this works at scale.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "nyofRGwnWzIlD5fpJXH8c",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2749.7309573585108,
      "y": 126.83181971307394,
      "width": 1261.9990234375,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b18",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1729468872,
      "version": 108,
      "versionNonce": 24807111,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782867643418,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why choosing Redis (in-memory DB for leaderboard)\n* Every submission that passes test cases updates that user's score. \nTheir rank might change. Everyone below them shifts down one position. \nAt peak, that's hundreds of score updates per second, each potentially reshuffling thousands of ranks. \nWe need fast writes (update scores) and fast reads (query top 50).\n\n* A SQL database would struggle. The query SELECT * FROM leaderboard ORDER BY score DESC LIMIT 50 \nscans and sorts the entire leaderboard on every request. With scores changing hundreds of times per second, \nthe ORDER BY never gets to use a stable index—it's recalculating constantly. \nWe need a data structure that maintains sorted order automatically.\n\n* in-memory data structure like Redis sorted sets. When you insert a score, \nRedis maintains order using a skip list internally—no separate sort step needed. \nQuerying top 50 is O(log n + 50), not O(n log n). Updates happen in O(log n). \nThis matches our access pattern: frequent writes, frequent sorted queries. We will discuss more on this in the deep dive section.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why choosing Redis (in-memory DB for leaderboard)\n* Every submission that passes test cases updates that user's score. \nTheir rank might change. Everyone below them shifts down one position. \nAt peak, that's hundreds of score updates per second, each potentially reshuffling thousands of ranks. \nWe need fast writes (update scores) and fast reads (query top 50).\n\n* A SQL database would struggle. The query SELECT * FROM leaderboard ORDER BY score DESC LIMIT 50 \nscans and sorts the entire leaderboard on every request. With scores changing hundreds of times per second, \nthe ORDER BY never gets to use a stable index—it's recalculating constantly. \nWe need a data structure that maintains sorted order automatically.\n\n* in-memory data structure like Redis sorted sets. When you insert a score, \nRedis maintains order using a skip list internally—no separate sort step needed. \nQuerying top 50 is O(log n + 50), not O(n log n). Updates happen in O(log n). \nThis matches our access pattern: frequent writes, frequent sorted queries. We will discuss more on this in the deep dive section.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
